WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.820
From TV to Ph.D., Mayim Bialik has truly&nbsp;
blossomed throughout the years. Here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.663
her transformation from childhood,&nbsp;
The Big Bang Theory, and beyond.

00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:15.120
Mayim Bialik's breakout role was the young&nbsp;
C.C. Bloom in the 1988 movie Beaches. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.080
audition process took four months, and she&nbsp;
figured there was no way she'd get the part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.060
Her hair was the wrong color, and at the age of&nbsp;
12, she saw that as a huge problem. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.560
all it took was a wig to alter her appearance.&nbsp;
And then the rest — the accent, the singing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.860
and the vivacious personality&nbsp;
— were all her. Now it's pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.340
much impossible to imagine anyone else&nbsp;
as the child version of Bette Midler.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.660
Between takes, Bialik was just like any other&nbsp;
kid, as she relished hamburgers for lunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.040
and took naps in her trailer on particularly&nbsp;
hot days. But when the cameras were rolling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.880
she was 100 percent on, delivering a&nbsp;
performance that won rave reviews and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.960
launched her into a life she'd never imagined.&nbsp;
As she recalled on her website in 2018,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.880
"It was this movie that changed my life&nbsp;
and catapulted me into working on sitcoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.440
which determined the course of my career and&nbsp;
life. Beaches is hard for me to watch because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.280
I'm awkward and my voice is annoying to&nbsp;
me and that's just the truth. But I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.701
so honored to have been a part of this movie&nbsp;
which has touched so many lives and hearts."

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:12.840
"My parents always said I look like&nbsp;
Bette Midler and Barbra Streisand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.469
so I'm trying to cover that demographic."

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:21.000
Bialik obviously didn't stop after Beaches. Soon&nbsp;
afterwards, she guest starred on the popular ABC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.020
sitcom Webster, as she played Frieda,&nbsp;
the title character's brainiac friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.880
for eight episodes. Then on the Fox sitcom&nbsp;
Molloy, she played the titular 11-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.200
who finds her entire world off its axis when&nbsp;
her mother dies and her father remarries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.180
moves her to New York. The show only aired for&nbsp;
seven episodes, with an additional three unaired.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.320
Bialik also had a few guest appearances on&nbsp;
a few other popular shows around this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.055
including Murphy Brown, Doogie Howser,&nbsp;
M.D., MacGyver, and Empty Nest.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.400
Beginning in 1990, Bialik starred as the&nbsp;
title character on the NBC sitcom Blossom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.760
which centered around a teenager living&nbsp;
with her father and two brothers after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.760
their mother leaves the family.&nbsp;
It tackled all sorts of issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.540
including single parenting, drug&nbsp;
abuse, sibling rivalry, and divorce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.820
and Bialik loved working on it with all of her&nbsp;
costars. As she recalled to US Weekly in 2021,

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.320
"This show was for sure an anomaly when we&nbsp;
came out in 1990. A show about a girl was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.980
unheard of on network TV at the time and&nbsp;
people thought we were nuts to try it."

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.960
Over the course of five seasons, Bialik grew&nbsp;
up on screen while she played Blossom. She's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.640
described the character as "unconventional,&nbsp;
quirky, smart, fun and fearless." Years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.880
the actress found that her character&nbsp;
reflected a lot of her own personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.560
as she aged. As she explained to US Weekly,&nbsp;
Blossom's introspection and ability to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.420
depth in any situation and person reflected&nbsp;
her own aspirations to do the same. She also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.680
speculated that Blossom would now be a civil&nbsp;
rights attorney or working with at-risk youth.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.700
"I think of Blossom sort of as professional.&nbsp;
I would assume she went to college."

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.100
"I can see her studying neuroscience&nbsp;
and ending up in a sitcom."

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:50.880
"Okay, well."

00:02:51.145 --> 00:02:55.980
Mayim Bialik was bright beyond her years when&nbsp;
she was a kid. That was abundantly clear when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.660
she was accepted into both Harvard and Yale&nbsp;
at the age of 17. She graduated from high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.460
school in 1993 and planned to put off pursuing&nbsp;
higher education until she finished Blossom.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.900
She ultimately didn't attend either of those&nbsp;
Ivy League schools, but clearly all her hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.220
work was paying off. She attributes her love&nbsp;
for science and drive to be accepted by these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.080
great schools to her biology tutor on the Blossom&nbsp;
set. As she revealed to Brain and Life in 2018,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.640
"I was a diligent student, but science&nbsp;
didn't come naturally to me. Because of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.860
I didn't think it was open to me as a career."

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.040
Bialik ultimately opted to attend&nbsp;
the University of California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.780
Los Angeles. She majored in neuroscience&nbsp;
and minored in Hebrew and Jewish studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.320
and she graduated in 2000. As&nbsp;
she revealed to Brain and Life,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.460
"I specifically fell in love with the action&nbsp;
potential and electrical properties of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.300
neuron when I was in my first semester&nbsp;
at UCLA. I love understanding the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.140
we think and feel and communicate — and&nbsp;
neuroscience is the science of all that."

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.900
After graduating, Bialik then pursued a doctorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.460
also in neuroscience and also at UCLA. She's&nbsp;
revealed that she wasn't at the top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.360
class in either undergrad or grad school,&nbsp;
but she persisted because of her love of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:05.400
science. She ultimately received her Ph.D. in&nbsp;
2007. As she later noted to Brain and Life,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.720
"I'm using my visibility to encourage girls&nbsp;
and young women to take an interest in science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.901
and give them a deeper understanding&nbsp;
of what they can do in that world."

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:17.290
"Yeah, I went to college. I went to graduate&nbsp;
school. I finished my doctorate last year."

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.080
It's no secret that Mayim Bialik took a&nbsp;
significant step away from acting while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.520
she was pursuing her neuroscience degrees. She&nbsp;
wanted to focus as much as possible on school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.020
but she didn't stop acting completely, as she&nbsp;
did voiceover work for plenty of cartoons.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.500
Her long list of animated credits&nbsp;
includes The Adventures of Hyperman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.180
The Real Adventures of Jonny Quest, Extreme&nbsp;
Ghostbusters, Johnny Bravo, Hey Arnold!,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.400
and Kim Possible. She also had longer stints&nbsp;
on Recess as Kirsten Kurst and Lloyd in Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.720
as Mean Cindy. After school, she gradually&nbsp;
eased her way back to live action roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.314
perhaps most notably with a recurring part&nbsp;
on The Secret Life of the American Teenager.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.860
Bialik's personal life was also busy during this&nbsp;
time. In August 2003, she married her husband Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.840
Stone, although they ended up divorcing in 2013.&nbsp;
During their time together, they had two sons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.880
named Miles and Frederick. And one of the reasons&nbsp;
Bialik got back into acting was so that she could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.960
spend more time with her boys. She knew that a&nbsp;
research professor job would have long hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.520
and acting actually would be less stressful in&nbsp;
that regard. Furthermore, her health insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.660
was running out. As she explained during an&nbsp;
appearance on the Canadian talk show The Social,

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.740
"I figured if I can just get even a&nbsp;
couple [acting] jobs like here or there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.940
I'll be able to get insurance again. I did not&nbsp;
expect to be a regular on a television show."

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.440
In 2010, Bialik landed the recurring role&nbsp;
of a high school guidance counselor with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.600
a quirky backstory on ABC Family's The Secret&nbsp;
Life of the American Teenager. Working with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.980
younger cast was quite the change of pace&nbsp;
for Bialik. As she confessed to PeopleTV,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.200
"It definitely felt like I was this old dinosaur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.200
coming in [...] I had a great time&nbsp;
being the old lady on the block."

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.100
Bialik appeared in six episodes of Secret&nbsp;
Life, and by the end of her time there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.751
she'd already booked her next big gig,&nbsp;
and it couldn't have been more perfect.

00:05:57.968 --> 00:06:02.220
Interestingly, Bialik's role as Amy Farrah&nbsp;
Fowler on The Big Bang Theory ended up being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.060
the perfect job for her to also be able&nbsp;
to focus on being a mother. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.140
when she joined the show in 2010 for&nbsp;
Season 3, she was meant to just be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.920
guest character. But it soon became clear&nbsp;
that Amy was the perfect love interest for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.855
Jim Parsons' Sheldon Cooper. So in Season&nbsp;
4, Bialik was promoted to series regular.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.320
"Now, before this goes any further, you&nbsp;
should know that all forms of physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.900
contact up to and including&nbsp;
coitus are off the table."

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:30.060
There are a lot of similarities between Amy and&nbsp;
the real-life Mayim. They're both neuroscience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.840
specialists, so Bialik was able to bring a&nbsp;
lot of real-life knowledge to the role and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.200
relate to her long hours of studying&nbsp;
the human brain. More than anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.680
Bialik appreciates that The Big Bang&nbsp;
Theory featured women in scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.700
and technical fields. As she put it&nbsp;
in a 2016 interview with Observer,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.749
"I'm really proud that I'm working [on] a&nbsp;
show that highlights women in this way."

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:53.160
Nowadays, Bialik is of course most well&nbsp;
known for Big Bang, but her acting career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:57.060
hasn't stopped there. She's continued to&nbsp;
voice act on a variety of animated series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.240
and she also played the character of&nbsp;
Glenda on an episode of the YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.920
series Rhett and Link's Buddy System. Plus,&nbsp;
she's reprised the role of Amy Farrah Fowler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.267
in voiceover form on two episodes of the&nbsp;
Big Bang Theory spinoff Young Sheldon.

00:07:09.267 --> 00:07:13.980
"I wanted his name to be Leonard Nimoy&nbsp;
Cooper, but Amy wouldn't let me."

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.140
"Be happy I let you name him Leonard!"

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.160
"Okay, okay."

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.960
In addition to all of these smaller roles, Bialik&nbsp;
has been starring as the title character on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:27.480
Fox sitcom Call Me Kat since January 2021.&nbsp;
The show follows Bialik as a woman who uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.440
her wedding savings to open a cat cafe. She&nbsp;
works to show everyone she doesn't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.460
find happiness or define herself through a&nbsp;
relationship and pursues her own interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.720
to be successful in her own way. Despite a&nbsp;
less-than-enthusiastic critical response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.257
the show has managed to keep on moving along, as&nbsp;
its third season premiered in the fall of 2022.

00:07:45.257 --> 00:07:49.800
Like plenty of other celebrities, Bialik has&nbsp;
authored or co-authored a handful of books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.720
to the point that she's even officially a New&nbsp;
York Times bestseller. She shared credit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:00.360
two of her books with pediatrician Dr. Jay Gordon.&nbsp;
In Beyond the Sling and Mayim's Vegan Table, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.500
incorporates Dr. Gordon's point of view to help&nbsp;
her direct her parenting tips and perspectives.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.660
Bialik is credited as the sole author of her&nbsp;
other two books. Girling Up is a short read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.460
about the hardships and realities of girls growing&nbsp;
up that uses science to explore how their bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.380
and minds change. Meanwhile, Boying Up has the&nbsp;
same mission as its predecessor, but for boys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.080
Both books feature a healthy&nbsp;
mix of science and emotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.980
attempting to ease the transition between&nbsp;
adolescence and adulthood. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.540
Bialik went through her own adolescence under&nbsp;
the watchful eye of a TV audience, so it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:35.000
sense that she would want to offer helpful tips to&nbsp;
youngsters going through their own awkward years.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.360
As if acting, neuroscience, parenting,&nbsp;
and writing weren't enough, Mayim Bialik&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.820
has also added podcasting and filmmaking to&nbsp;
her resume. In January 2021, she launched a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.380
podcast called Mayim Bialik's Breakdown that&nbsp;
focuses on mental health and wellness. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:53.820
and her co-host Jonathan Cohen hope to change&nbsp;
popular misconceptions about mental health.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.680
The show's primary topic and Bialik's status&nbsp;
as a popular TV star have helped them book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:04.020
an eclectic array of guests, including Alanis&nbsp;
Morissette, Charlamagne tha God, and Steve-O.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:08.700
As for Bialik working behind the camera, her&nbsp;
debut as a feature film writer and director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:14.220
As They Made Us, was released in April 2022. A&nbsp;
bittersweet story of a young woman coping with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:19.140
a divorce and the oncoming death of her father,&nbsp;
the movie stars Dianna Agron, Dustin Hoffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:24.300
and Bialik's Big Bang costar Simon Helberg.&nbsp;
In an interview with Variety, Bialik revealed,

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.020
"I wrote the way a lot of writers write&nbsp;
— because we feel compelled to. It comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:32.254
from something that needs to live on paper,&nbsp;
instead of just in our heads or in our hearts."

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:38.164
"I didn't necessarily want to write it. I felt&nbsp;
a need to write some of these things down."

00:09:38.292 --> 00:09:42.960
Another huge way that Mayim Bialik puts her&nbsp;
smarts to fine use is as one of the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.960
co-hosts of the popular game show Jeopardy! After&nbsp;
the death of longtime emcee Alex Trebek in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.100
Jeopardy! had some massive shoes to fill.&nbsp;
Before choosing an official full-time hire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.940
the show trotted out a variety of&nbsp;
temporary guest hosts, including Bialik.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.840
After much deliberation and some strong&nbsp;
fan opinions, it was announced in July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.580
2022 that Bialik would split hosting duties&nbsp;
with Ken Jennings, one of Jeopardy!'s all-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.840
greatest champions. Jennings was set to&nbsp;
host from September to December 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.440
while Bialik would preside over the&nbsp;
Celebrity Jeopardy! primetime episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.300
and then take over from Jennings in January.&nbsp;
As Executive Producer Michael Davies raved,

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.920
"Mayim and Ken are both extraordinarily&nbsp;
talented and simply lovely humans. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.634
support the staff and each other. They love and&nbsp;
respect this institution of a television program."

00:10:27.634 --> 00:10:31.800
"This is the podium that has a little step&nbsp;
stool for me so that I'm as tall as Ken."

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.920
"And for me, it's got a little squishy thing,&nbsp;
'cause I have fallen arches. I got flat feet."

